      "text_raw": "How about the pole at\nnode 5?\nRemember that this is a\nnode at the other side of a\nsingle-ended amplifier. Each\ntime we have a single-ended\namplifier, the capacitances\non the other side than the\noutput, will be negligible for\nthe Phase Margin.\nIndeed, a pole-zero\ndoublet is created by the\ncapacitances at this node 5.\nIts spreading is only 2. As a\nresult, the effect on the\nPhase Margin is now\nnegligible.\nDespite the fact that we have three nodes at the 1/g level, we only have one single non-\nm\ndominant pole!",
      "text": "How about the pole at node 5? Remember that this is a node at the other side of a single-ended amplifier. Each time we have a single-ended amplifier, the capacitances on the other side than the output, will be negligible for the Phase Margin. Indeed, a pole-zero doublet is created by the capacitances at this node 5. Its spreading is only 2. As a result, the effect on the Phase Margin is now negligible. Despite the fact that we have three nodes at the 1/g level, we only have one single nonm dominant pole!"
